  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Anomalously cool this afternoon, with showers\nending by early evening. Clearing skies tonight, but with patchy\nfog and low stratus possible overnight into Sunday morning.\n\nCool sfc high pressure/CAD in place across the region this\nafternoon, with temperatures across southern VA and NE NC mostly\nin the upper 60s to lower 70s. This could be cool enough to\nchallenge daily record low max temperatures at ORF/ECG (see\nClimate section for details). Otherwise, showers are becoming\nconfined to far SE VA and NE NC this afternoon, and will\ncontinue to slide off to the SE and offshore by sunset. To the\nnorth, skies have already partially cleared and this should\nspread south into most of the CWA during the evening hrs.\nHowever, with little to no wind overnight and lingering low\nlevel moisture, expect to see low stratus and at least patchy\nfog develop, especially over the piedmont (though fog will be\npossible farther east as well). Temperatures don't drop off too\nmuch tonight, with lows generally in the low- mid 60s (upper \n60s SE). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Isolated/widely scattered late day showers/storms \nSunday/Monday, with higher rain chances Tuesday into Wednesday;\nstrong to severe storms are possible Tuesday. \n\nTemperatures trend closer to normal on Sunday as weak sfc high\npressure settles across the area, with an upper level ridge\namplifying across the central and SW CONUS. Light onshore flow\nwill keep highs in the lower 80s along the coast, with mid to\nupper 80s elsewhere. Dew P's stay reasonably comfortable by \nJuly standards (mostly in the 60s), under a partly-mostly sunny \nsky. The next shortwave aloft (currently located over NW\nOntario), dives SE and is forecast to push across the local area\nlate Sunday/Sunday night. Models show some varying solutions as\nto the highest coverage of showers/storms, and for now a blended\nsolution yields 30-40% chc PoPs along and W of I-95, tapered to\n~20% closer to the coast. We are not outlooked by SPC for any\nSVR threat, and the timing mostly getting close to sunset or\nlater would further limit a widespread SVR threat, but can't \nrule out an isolated strong storm over the piedmont given decent\nshear in the tightening NW flow aloft. NW flow pattern continues\nMonday, with the potential for additional shortwaves (though\ntiming these features beyond 24 hrs always becomes challenging).\nWill again have chc PoPs for most of the area later Monday into\nMonday evening, again with a strong/SVR storm or two not out of\nthe question despite no SPC outlook. The airmass gradually\nbecomes more humid with dew P's Monday in the upper 60s to \nlower 70s coupled with high temperatures from the mid 80s to \naround 90F. Latest models continue to suggest Tuesday has the \nbetter SVR threat, as well as a higher storm coverage as a \nstrong cold front and upper level trough/shortwave move into the\narea from the NW. The operational ECMWF continues to be most \naggressive with the upper tough (actually becoming cutoff over \nthe NE and northern mid-Atlantic) while the GFS/Canadian are a \nbit more progressive. Shear looks impressive by July standards, \nand the models mostly depict decent instability and even mid \nlevel lapse rates up to 6.0-6.5C, which would support a robust \ndamaging wind and hail threat. As always though, timing of the \nstorms will be important to how much severe WX occurs (as has \nbeen the case with some recent events). Highs Tuesday look to be\nin the upper 80s to around  90F, along with humid conditions \n9Dew P's in low-mid 70s). SPC still has the FA highlighted in \nthe extended severe outlook. The NBM has fairly low PoPs for \nWednesday, though some additional showers/storms are possible \nagain especially if the more amplified solutions verify.",
